title: The Inauguration
new-page: true
tags: event

The first day of the new year. It was almost noon. Staring at the towers reflecting the light of the winter sun, rays bleached all in one monochrome hue.

Last night, you heard the fireworks blaring louder. The people have high hopes for you. Or at least, hope that you won’t be as terrible as the previous administration.

Those days pressed on you. Getting crushed by the concrete of an entire city. It lingers. Your eyes struggle to stay open.

“Are you well rested, sir?”

“Yes, Alfred.”
 
He had taken a seat in front. You didn’t ask him to come with you, but he made a compelling case for why he should. You don’t know what made you even consider the contrary.

Usually, families followed new mayors. You had no wife or parents to bring. Still, they both took time to follow you.

“You look worse than usual, Bruce. Are you alright?”

Snapping your eyes awake. You turned to face Dick. Even with his strange ascetic ways at his home, it seems he kept the taste for haute couture you cultivated in him.

“Yes,” you spoke. “With you there, I will.”

Even as you tried, ash pushed through your teeth. No words, only sounds. There was an empty seat across from you. No one.

“Thank you. Thank you for being here.”

You let the moment sit. It comes in waves, yet here, you know you can control it.

Opening the door to the blinding lights. The white snow reflecting off the rays above, dark-dressed men and women standing around you. They stared at each part of you. You knew to give a smile, to wave back. You had rehearsed how not to make it feel rehearsed.

Seeing all those across from you. You know the faces of some of them. Some you’ve met with another visage. You smile, the people of Gotham know your loss. They know your pain.

Is it a white lie to tell them you’re already feeling better? Telling them a lie so they can hope that they can find it too. You didn’t give them the full truth. And yet, they voted for you.

Standing on the podium. You had taken a few papers with you. Looking at the crowd, a mic below your mouth, it finally hit you. You hadn’t said a word. They looked at every step you took, interpreting every twitch of your mouth. Every letter matters in the story they wish to hear.

